1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,300
Well, you've both been looking at different styles of managing individuals in companies

2
00:00:06,300 --> 00:00:07,283
and the workplace.

3
00:00:07,283 --> 00:00:09,650
How's the research going, Philip?

4
00:00:09,650 --> 00:00:16,245
Well, I've been looking at why individualism, I mean individual differences, are such an

5
00:00:16,245 --> 00:00:18,600
important area of management studies.

6
00:00:18,600 --> 00:00:23,114
When you think about any organization, be it a family business

7
00:00:23,114 --> 00:00:28,860
or a multinational company, they are all fundamentally a group of people working together.

8
00:00:28,860 --> 00:00:33,031
But it's what these individuals contribute to their places of work that makes you

9
00:00:33,031 --> 00:00:34,520
realize how important they are.

10
00:00:34,520 --> 00:00:36,886
Of course they bring different ideas,

11
00:00:36,886 --> 00:00:40,830
but it's also their attitudes and their experiences of learning.

12
00:00:40,830 --> 00:00:43,800
Diversity is important in these areas too.

13
00:00:43,800 --> 00:00:48,460
So why do people behave so differently from one another at work?

14
00:00:48,460 --> 00:00:53,755
There are lots of reasons but research has shown a lot of it comes

15
00:00:53,755 --> 00:00:54,890
down to personality.

16
00:00:54,890 --> 00:00:57,280
And the other factor is gender.

17
00:00:57,280 --> 00:01:01,582
It's a well known fact that men and women do lots of things in

18
00:01:01,582 --> 00:01:02,196
different ways,

19
00:01:02,196 --> 00:01:04,040
and the workplace is no different.

20
00:01:04,040 --> 00:01:08,050
Did you look at the effects of this variation on companies?

21
00:01:08,050 --> 00:01:09,298
Yes, I did.

22
00:01:09,298 --> 00:01:15,120
On the positive side, exposure to such diversity helps encourage creativity which is generally

23
00:01:15,120 --> 00:01:17,200
an asset to a company.

24
00:01:17,200 --> 00:01:22,130
But unfortunately individual differences are also the root of conflict between staff

25
00:01:22,130 --> 00:01:25,416
and they can lead to difficulties for management,

26
00:01:25,416 --> 00:01:27,470
which can sometimes be serious.

27
00:01:27,470 --> 00:01:28,363
Thanks, Philip.

28
00:01:28,363 --> 00:01:35,507
So now I guess the two main things to remember here are to identify individual talent

29
00:01:35,507 --> 00:01:37,740
and then to utilize it.

30
00:01:37,740 --> 00:01:43,370
So Janice, you were looking at identifying different talents in workers.

31
00:01:43,370 --> 00:01:46,260
Do you think this is easy for managers to do?

32
00:01:46,260 --> 00:01:52,387
Well, currently teamwork is in fashion in the workplace and in my opinion the

33
00:01:52,387 --> 00:01:55,450
importance of the individual is generally neglected.

34
00:01:55,450 --> 00:02:01,727
What managers should be targeting is those employees who can take the lead in a situation

35
00:02:01,727 --> 00:02:05,650
and are not afraid to accept the idea of responsibility.

36
00:02:05,650 --> 00:02:12,350
That's true Janice but unfortunately many managers think the entire notion of encouraging individuality

37
00:02:12,350 --> 00:02:15,700
amongst their staff is far too hard.

38
00:02:15,700 --> 00:02:16,062
Yes,

39
00:02:16,062 --> 00:02:21,126
that may be true but I think one of the most important tasks of

40
00:02:21,126 --> 00:02:26,551
managers is to consider the needs of the individual on one hand and group co-operation

41
00:02:26,551 --> 00:02:28,360
and conformity on the other.

42
00:02:28,360 --> 00:02:33,000
It requires creative thinking on the part of management to avoid tension.

43
00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:38,130
So Janice, what kind of people do you think companies should be looking for?

44
00:02:38,130 --> 00:02:43,616
Well, it has to start from the very beginning when companies are looking for

45
00:02:43,616 --> 00:02:44,400
new employees.

46
00:02:44,400 --> 00:02:51,381
When the personnel department is choosing between applicants they need to look for someone who's broken the mould

47
00:02:51,381 --> 00:02:53,320
and can think for themselves.

48
00:02:53,320 --> 00:02:59,138
Instead, people making these decisions often use a range of psychological tests to see

49
00:02:59,138 --> 00:03:02,047
if a person is a problem solver,

50
00:03:02,047 --> 00:03:04,540
or will do as they're told.

51
00:03:04,540 --> 00:03:09,000
I'm not convinced these qualities are actually the most important.

52
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:12,800
So do you think being a good team player is overrated?

53
00:03:12,800 --> 00:03:14,351
NO, it's not overrated.

54
00:03:14,351 --> 00:03:18,617
You do need to learn the rules and learn them fast.

55
00:03:18,617 --> 00:03:23,270
No individual can get around this if you're working in an organization.

56
00:03:23,270 --> 00:03:26,000
So how should managers deal with this?

57
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:26,559
Rewards.

58
00:03:26,559 --> 00:03:34,391
When an individual demonstrates the behaviour the organisation expects, some kind of incentive can

59
00:03:34,391 --> 00:03:35,510
be given.

60
00:03:35,510 --> 00:03:39,329
What's important here is that this happens right at the beginning

61
00:03:39,329 --> 00:03:42,800
so new recruits learn the rules of the system immediately.

62
00:03:42,800 --> 00:03:47,425
Also the incentive should be something the individual actually wants,

63
00:03:47,425 --> 00:03:50,200
and this isn't always just money.

64
00:03:50,200 --> 00:03:52,728
To come back to you, Philip.

65
00:03:52,728 --> 00:03:56,941
You were saying that recognition of good performers is essential.

66
00:03:56,941 --> 00:03:59,890
What else should managers be looking for?

67
00:03:59,890 --> 00:04:05,882
Well, managing people means you not only have an understanding of your employees,

68
00:04:05,882 --> 00:04:10,030
but you also recognise the culture of the organization.

69
00:04:10,030 --> 00:04:15,259
In fact, for some organizations creativity and individuality may be the last thing they

70
00:04:15,259 --> 00:04:17,500
want to see during working hours!

71
00:04:17,500 --> 00:04:18,520
Very true.

72
00:04:18,520 --> 00:04:18,910
Yes,

73
00:04:18,910 --> 00:04:22,420
but managing people isn't as easy as it looks.

74
00:04:22,420 --> 00:04:27,857
For example, change in the workplace can be quite tricky, especially if there's a

75
00:04:27,857 --> 00:04:29,410
need to increase profit.

76
00:04:29,410 --> 00:04:35,811
And at times like these managers may have to give priority to profit rather

77
00:04:35,811 --> 00:04:37,640
than individual staff needs.

78
00:04:37,640 --> 00:04:38,091
Yes,

79
00:04:38,091 --> 00:04:41,250
and that creates difficult situations for people.

80
00:04:41,250 --> 00:04:46,344
Yes but what's important is that managers are able to deal with quite high

81
00:04:46,344 --> 00:04:47,800
levels of personal stress.

82
00:04:47,800 --> 00:04:53,327
During times of change they should be thinking not only about the strain on their staff

83
00:04:53,327 --> 00:04:56,090
but take time out to think of themselves.

84
00:04:56,090 --> 00:04:56,498
Absolutely.

85
00:04:56,498 --> 00:04:59,760
So what are the implications of that for...
